          "deepDive": "**〜に加えて** (加える = to add) literally means *adding to ~*. The noun before it is the first item; the main clause adds a second one of the **same type or direction** — two costs, two strengths, two problems. The main clause often contains も or a list (〜や〜など).\n\n- **に加え** is the written clause-linking form.\n- **それに加えて** is used at the start of a sentence: *in addition to that*.\n\nNeighbors and differences:\n- **〜だけでなく / 〜ばかりでなく**: everyday equivalents; can attach to verbs and adjectives, while に加えて attaches to **nouns** (a clause needs こと: 雨が多いことに加えて).\n- **〜上（に）** (#102): *on top of ~*, usually two facts in the same direction, often piling up bad or good things: 熱が出た上に下痢が止まらない. It connects to clauses directly.\n- **〜のみならず** (#63): formal *not only ~*; widens the range of people/things affected.\n- **〜はもちろん**: *not to mention ~* — the first item is obvious.\n\nCommon mistake: using it to contrast things. ✗給料が安いに加えて仕事は楽だ — に加えて adds, it doesn't contrast (use が/一方で). Also avoid ✗安いに加えて: an adjective needs こと or the noun form 安さ.\n\nJLPT tip: when a passage lists costs or burdens (建設費に加えて運営費も…), に加えて is the typical connector; in 問題2, the piece に加えて usually sits between two parallel nouns.",
